-Note on allowed operations
---------------------------
-Library sequence-scan primitives that would perform the whole recurrence in one call
-are out of scope — the scan is built explicitly. The current implementation walks the
-time axis in a plain Python ``for`` loop, one timestep per iteration, so it issues
-O(L) dependent kernel launches and serializes on the time dimension. It is correct
-but leaves the sequence-level parallelism of the recurrence on the table.
+Implementation notes
+--------------------
+The recurrence is evaluated with a chunked ("blocked") scan built from primitive
+ops in custom Triton kernels — no library scan primitive is used:
+
+1. Kernel 1 (parallel over batch x channel-block x time-chunk): marches one chunk
+   of ``CL`` timesteps with the running state held in registers, emitting the
+   per-chunk transition product ``P_g = prod A`` and the zero-init local end
+   state ``H_g``.
+2. Kernel 2 (sequential over the ``G = ceil(L / CL)`` chunks only): combines the
+   chunk summaries into the true state at each chunk boundary,
+   ``S_{g+1} = P_g * S_g + H_g``.
+3. Kernel 3 (parallel like kernel 1): re-marches each chunk, reconstructing
+   ``h[t] = (cum A up to t) * S_g + h_local[t]`` exactly as the unrolled
+   recurrence dictates, and writes ``y[t] = sum_n C[t, n] * h[t]``.
+
+This keeps the strict causal state-transition order of the math while replacing
+O(L) dependent kernel launches with O(1) launches and exposing parallelism along
+batch, channels, and time chunks.
